+# Provisional database model
+
+Revision 1 — 2026-10-02. This page owns relationship assumptions, the canonical ER
+view and open model decisions. [Glossary](../project/glossary.md) owns meanings;
+[scenarios](scenarios.md) exercise retrieval results.
+
+## Scope and evidence
+
+The [brief](../../brief.md) supplies organisation, agreement and service, and asks
+for agreements involving an organisation and the service each agreement covers.
+[Project instructions](../../AGENTS.md) select PostgreSQL and limit this work to
+design. No schema is implemented or adopted by this proposal.
+
+## Trial assumptions
+
+- A1: each agreement involves exactly one organisation; an organisation can
+  involve zero or more agreements. This deliberately small hypothesis lets us
+  trace the first question. It is not evidence that agreements have one party.
+- A2: each agreement covers exactly one service; a service can be covered by zero
+  or more agreements. The singular wording motivates this hypothesis but does
+  not settle cardinality.
+- A3: each entity has a stable identity independent of its display name or
+  external reference. Renaming preserves its relationships. Synthetic labels in
+  the scenarios stand for identities, not a chosen key format.
+
+## Canonical ER view
+
+Conceptual, provisional view under A1–A3. Every multiplicity below is an assumption,
+not an adopted constraint. No attributes, data types or foreign keys are implied.
+
+```mermaid
+erDiagram
+    ORGANISATION ||--o{ AGREEMENT : "involved in (A1)"
+    SERVICE ||--o{ AGREEMENT : "covered by (A2)"
+```
+
+The three entities remain atomic here because neither retrieval question requires
+internal structure. Participant capacity belongs to the organisation's involvement
+in an agreement; it cannot safely be inferred from the organisation itself.
+No roles are assigned in this trial. “Involved” means only the recorded association;
+it establishes no ownership, payer status or operational permission.
+
+For question 1, match organisation identity and follow its involvement to agreement
+identities. For question 2, follow each agreement's coverage to service identity.
+Names label results; they do not control matching.
+
+## Decisions blocking implementation
+
+1. Decide what involvement includes, its permitted roles and role scope, whether
+   several organisations may participate, whether one may hold several roles,
+   and whether an agreement may have no known participant. A1 cannot represent
+   a second organisation or distinguish capacities. A participant association
+   would be a candidate if those requirements are confirmed, not an adopted entity.
+2. Decide whether coverage permits zero, one or several services and whether a
+   service may exist without agreements. A2 cannot represent missing or multiple
+   coverage. Do not discard such evidence to fit this diagram.
+3. Confirm identity and duplicate-detection rules, including the scope and
+   reliability of external references. PostgreSQL key types, uniqueness,
+   nullability and relationship representation depend on these decisions.
+4. Define whether corrected or withdrawn agreements must remain retrievable and
+   what historical relationships must survive. This exercise assigns current
+   involvement and coverage to the agreement; it selects no deletion, cascade,
+   temporal or update behavior.
+
+The next bounded modeling step is to resolve items 1–2 with domain evidence and
+revise affected scenarios. Application stack, packaging, migrations and runtime
+validation remain outside this proposal.
