+# Retrieval scenarios
+
+Revision 1 — 2026-10-02. This page owns synthetic examples and expected answers.
+They reason about the [provisional model](model.md); they are not executable tests,
+seed data or evidence about PostgreSQL behavior.
+
+## Ordinary retrieval and absence
+
+Assume organisations O1 “Amber Orchard” and O2 “Cobalt Meadow”; services S1
+“Lantern care” and S2 “Canopy inspection”; agreements A1 “Spring accord” and A2
+“Autumn accord”. Both agreements involve O1. A1 covers S1; A2 covers S2.
+All identifiers and names are fabricated.
+
+- Question 1 for O1 returns A1 and A2, through organisation → involvement →
+  agreement. For O2 it returns no agreements. This exercises A1's zero-or-more
+  agreements per organisation without implying an agreement can lack a participant.
+- Question 2 returns A1 → S1 and A2 → S2 through agreement → coverage → service.
+  This exercises model assumption A2.
+
+## Identity despite matching labels
+
+Add O3, also named “Amber Orchard”, with no agreements. Question 1 for O3 returns
+none, while O1 still returns A1 and A2. Renaming O1 to “Amber Orchard Collective”
+also preserves its answer. Following identity, not a name match, exercises A3.
+
+## Second participant and roles
+
+Suppose evidence says O2 also participates in A1, while O1 has two capacities in
+A1. Under a meaning of involvement that includes both organisations, question 1
+for O2 should include A1, once, regardless of role count. The current model cannot
+represent this case. Which capacities count, whether two organisations are allowed,
+and how roles attach remain unresolved in model decision 1. This counterexample
+challenges A1; it does not silently adopt many-to-many participation.
+
+## Missing or multiple service coverage
+
+Suppose A1 covers both S1 and S2. If both coverage facts are valid, question 2 must
+return both; assumption A2 cannot express that result. If an agreement has no known
+service, its expected treatment is unresolved: incomplete information and forbidden
+absence are different rules. Model decision 2 must settle both cases before schema
+constraints or final expected results can be claimed.
